 Creating a complex smart contract involves writing a self-executing program on a blockchain that automates the enforcement of an agreement between parties. The complexity of a smart contract can vary greatly depending on the specific use case, the blockchain platform being used, and the level of detail required.

Here's a simplified example of a complex smart contract for a real estate transaction on the Ethereum blockchain:

```solidity
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract RealEstateContract {
    address public buyer;
    address public seller;
    uint public propertyId;
    uint public price;
    bool public isPurchased = false;

    constructor(uint _propertyId, uint _price) public {
        require(_propertyId > 0, "Property ID must be greater than 0.");
        require(_price > 0, "Price must be greater than 0.");
        buyer = msg.sender;
        seller = address(0);
        propertyId = _propertyId;
        price = _price;
    }

    function sell(address _seller, uint _newPrice) public {
        require(msg.sender == seller, "Only the seller can sell the property.");
        require(_newPrice > price, "New price must be greater than the current price.");
        seller = address(0);
        price = _newPrice;
    }

    function buy(uint _deposit) public payable {
        require(msg.value == _deposit, "Deposit amount must match the specified amount.");
        require(!isPurchased, "Property has already been purchased.");
        require(msg.sender != seller, "The seller cannot buy the property.");
        require(msg.sender.send(_deposit * 0.01 ether) == true, "The buyer does not have enough Ether to send the deposit.");
        isPurchased = true;
    }

    function completePurchase() public {
        require(isPurchased, "Property has not been purchased.");
        require(msg.sender == buyer, "Only the buyer can complete the purchase.");
        require(msg.sender.send(price - buyer.call.value(this).balanceOf(buyer)) == true, "The buyer does not have enough Ether to complete the purchase.");
        seller.transfer(price);
        isPurchased = false;
    }
}
```

This smart contract allows for the sale and purchase of a property, with the ability for the seller to increase the price before the contract is completed. The buyer must deposit a certain amount to show their intent to purchase, and the contract will automatically complete the purchase once the deposit and the remaining balance are transferred.

This is a simplified example, and a real-world smart contract for a real estate transaction would likely be more complex, including additional features such as escrow services, timelines, and dispute resolution mechanisms. It's also important to note that smart contracts should be thoroughly tested and audited before deployment to ensure they function as intended.

